# Q: is the sky in the right place, at the right time, from the right place?
#
# The sky is a property of the simulation rather than a texture, which is what makes it checkable.
# This probe dumps every star above the horizon in altitude and azimuth, with magnitude and colour,
# plus the Sun and the planets, plus a grid of galactic latitude for the Milky Way.
#
#   dotnet run -c Release --project src/SolSystem.Probe -- \
#       --probe tools/probe/sky.probe --probe-out artifacts/probe/sky.txt
#   python3 tools/draw_sky.py /tmp/sky.csv --out /tmp/sky.svg --title "..."
#
# The chart it draws can be checked against a planisphere, which is the only test of a sky that
# means anything: the tests in SkyTests check the frames, the sidereal time and the two arcsecond-
# scale effects, and the chart is what catches everything they cannot think of.

# Greenwich, 2000 January 1, 12:00 TT -- local noon in midwinter.
sky 51.4778 -0.0015 /tmp/sky-greenwich-winter.csv

# Half a year later, which is the same place at midnight in midsummer.
days 182.6
sky 51.4778 -0.0015 /tmp/sky-greenwich-summer.csv

# And the other hemisphere, where the sky is upside down and the Milky Way is at its richest.
sky -33.8688 151.2093 /tmp/sky-sydney.csv
